Notebook[{
Cell["DiagramToExpression: a programmer's manual", "Title"],
Cell["The Wolfram functions of CCN String Diagrams that turn boxes, lines and arrows into expressions", "Subtitle"],
Cell["By Dara O Shayda and Noor Hoshino (\:661f\:91ce\:30cc\:30fc\:30eb) \:00b7 \:00a9 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"],
Cell["After Turaev & Virelizier, Monoidal Categories and Topological Field Theory, Chapter 2. The package is diagram-to-expression.wl, in the same folder as this notebook (Desktop/Research/Monoidal Graphical Calculus). It is self-contained: it needs neither the simplify notebook nor any other engine file.", "Text"],
Cell["1. Loading the package", "Section"],
Cell["Evaluate the cell below once per session. The package lives in the context DiagramToExpression`, so its names do not collide with the engine's (which live in Global` in the notebooks and in GraphicalCalculusKernel` in the kernel). After loading, the public names are on $ContextPath. The private helpers are in DiagramToExpression`Private`.", "Text"],
Cell["Get[FileNameJoin[{$HomeDirectory, \"Desktop\", \"Research\", \"Monoidal Graphical Calculus\", \"diagram-to-expression.wl\"}]]", "Input"],
Cell["Every public symbol has a usage message. For example:", "Text"],
Cell["?toExpression", "Input"],
Cell["Names[\"DiagramToExpression`*\"]", "Input"],
Cell["2. Overview: four representations and the maps between them", "Section"],
Cell["A string diagram exists in this project in four forms. The translators convert between them, and three of them end in an algebraic expression:", "Text"],
Cell["StringDiagram[boxes, strands]: a drawn diagram, with coordinates. This is what the notebooks render, and what the reference translator toExpression reads.", "Item"],
Cell["LD[src, layers]: the layered form, one box per layer. This is what the rewrite rules work on, in Wolfram and in the editor's JavaScript.", "Item"],
Cell["Penrose strips {strip1, strip2, ...}: a diagram cut into horizontal strips of boxes and vertical segments, as in \:00a72.2.2 of the book.", "Item"],
Cell["JSON <|\"src\" -> ..., \"ls\" -> ...|>: the layered form as the editor sends it to the GraphicalCalculusKernel, with box names as strings.", "Item"],
Cell["The maps:", "Text"],
Cell["StringDiagram --toExpression------------------------> expression\nStringDiagram --toLayered---> LD --ldToExpression-------> expression\nLD --fromLayered--> StringDiagram    (layeredExpression = toExpression @ fromLayered)\nstrips --penroseLD--> LD             strips --penroseExpr--> expression\nJSON --jsonToLD--> LD --ldToJSON--> JSON          jsonToExpression = ldToExpression @ jsonToLD\nexpression --toEditorText--> text the editor's parser reads back", "Program"],
Cell["An expression is built from CircleTimes (\:2297), SmallCircle (\:2218), Subscript[\"id\", X] (identities) and unit (\|01d7d9). Morphism symbols are strings (\"f\") or the structured symbols of section 3.3, for example Subscript[\"ev\", \"X\"]. Composition follows the book: g \:2218 f means first f, then g.", "Text"],
Cell["3. Conventions", "Section"],
Cell["3.1 Reading direction and coordinates", "Subsection"],
Cell["Diagrams read bottom to top, as in the book: sources at the bottom, targets at the top. In g \:2218 f the box f is below the box g.", "Item"],
Cell["In a StringDiagram, x grows to the right and y grows upwards. A box MBox[name, symbol, {x1, x2}, y] has its centre at height y and is hgt = 0.5 tall, so its bottom edge is at y \:2212 0.25 and its top edge at y + 0.25.", "Item"],
Cell["Left to right is the order of the tensor product: the strand with the smallest x is the leftmost factor.", "Item"],
Cell["3.2 Colours, the unit and arrows", "Subsection"],
Cell["A colour is an object, written as a string: \"X\", \"Y*\", \"\:2228X\". A tensor colour such as \"X\:2297Y\" is one strand coloured by the product. The rules split it into strands, but the translators leave it as it is.", "Item"],
Cell["unit is the unit object \|01d7d9. tensor[{}] = unit, and strands coloured unit are dropped by toExpression and ldToExpression, since the book does not draw them.", "Item"],
Cell["Arrows (\:00a72.1.3). In a pivotal category the arcs are oriented, and the orientation is part of the colour: an arc coloured X points down, against the reading direction, and an arc coloured X* points up. No separate arrow data is stored. arrowOf[colour] reads the arrow off the colour (section 5). In \:00a72.1.2 notation, a left dual \:2228X is also drawn pointing up.", "Item"],
Cell["3.3 Names of the built-in morphisms", "Subsection"],
Cell["The structured symbols, with the editor's names (section 11 converts between them):", "Text"],
Cell["Subscript[\"ev\", X]                     ev_X      : X* \:2297 X \:2192 \|01d7d9   (\:2228X \:2297 X \:2192 \|01d7d9 in \:00a72.1.2)\nSubscript[\"coev\", X]                   coev_X    : \|01d7d9 \:2192 X \:2297 X*\nSubscript[OverTilde[\"ev\"], X]          ev~_X     : X \:2297 X* \:2192 \|01d7d9   (\:1ebdv)\nSubscript[OverTilde[\"coev\"], X]        coev~_X   : \|01d7d9 \:2192 X* \:2297 X   (c\:0303oev)\nSubscript[\"\:03c4\", \"X,Y\"]                  \:03c4_{X,Y}   : X \:2297 Y \:2192 Y \:2297 X (the braiding, X over Y)\nSubscript[Superscript[\"\:03c4\",\"-1\"], \"Y,X\"] \:03c4\:207b\:00b9_{Y,X} : X \:2297 Y \:2192 Y \:2297 X (the inverse of \:03c4_{Y,X})\nSubscript[\"\:03c8\", X], Subscript[Superscript[\"\:03c8\",\"-1\"], X]   \:03c8_X, \:03c8\:207b\:00b9_X (\:00a72.3.1)\nSubscript[\"\:03b8\:02e1\", X], Subscript[\"\:03b8\:02b3\:207b\:00b9\", X], ...              \:03b8\:02e1_X, \:03b8\:02b3\:207b\:00b9_X (twists, \:00a73.3.1)\n\"f\", \"f*\", \"p_\:03b1\", \"\:03c6_{i,j}\", ...       any other box: a plain string", "Program"],
Cell["4. Data structures", "Section"],
Cell["StringDiagram", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["StringDiagram[{MBox[...], ...}, {Strand[...], ...}]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["boxes: a list of MBox[name, symbol, {x1, x2}, y].", "Item"],
Cell["strands: a list of Strand[colour, x, lower, upper].", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["An inert head: a drawn diagram. Nothing evaluates.", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Each strand is one straight vertical segment, from its lower end to its upper end. A line that passes through several boxes is several strands.", "Item"],
Cell["Strands refer to boxes by name, so names must be unique. fromLayered names its boxes \"b1\", \"b2\", ... in layer order.", "Item"],
Cell["Crossings are not represented. The order of strands at any height is read from their x coordinates.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["d1 = StringDiagram[{MBox[\"f\", \"f\", {-0.3, 0.3}, 0]}, {Strand[\"X\", 0, Loose[-1], BottomEdge[\"f\"]], Strand[\"Y\", 0, TopEdge[\"f\"], Loose[1]]}]", "Input"],
Cell["MBox", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["MBox[name, symbol, {x1, x2}, y]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["name: a string, used by Strand ends (TopEdge[name], BottomEdge[name]).", "Item"],
Cell["symbol: the morphism, a string or a structured symbol (section 3.3).", "Item"],
Cell["{x1, x2}: the left and right edges. toExpression orders boxes and strands in a slice by x1 for boxes and x for strands.", "Item"],
Cell["y: the height of the centre.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["An inert head.", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The height of every box is hgt = 0.5. Two boxes with the same y are in the same slice for toExpression.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["hgt", "Input"],
Cell["Strand", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Strand[colour, x, lower, upper]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["colour: the object the strand is coloured by.", "Item"],
Cell["x: the horizontal position.", "Item"],
Cell["lower: the bottom end, Loose[y] (a source end at height y) or TopEdge[name] (the strand leaves the top of that box).", "Item"],
Cell["upper: the top end, Loose[y] (a target end) or BottomEdge[name] (the strand enters the bottom of that box).", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["An inert head.", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["endY[d, end] gives the height of an end. A strand passes a height y when endY[lower] < y < endY[upper], strictly.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["endY[d1, #] & /@ {Loose[-1], TopEdge[\"f\"], BottomEdge[\"f\"]}", "Input"],
Cell["LD and B", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["LD[src, {{a1, B[symbol1, ins1, outs1]}, {a2, B[...]}, ...}]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["src: the list of source colours, left to right.", "Item"],
Cell["a: the number of strands to the left of the box at the moment it is applied (0 = leftmost).", "Item"],
Cell["B[symbol, ins, outs]: the box, with its list of input colours and its list of output colours.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Inert heads. rows[ld] gives the colours between the layers, and validQ[ld] checks that every layer fits.", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The layers are listed bottom to top. Layer k replaces positions a+1 \:2026 a+Length[ins] of the current row by outs.", "Item"],
Cell["The form is the same in the notebooks (sections 14\:201319 of graphical-calculus-2.1-simplify.nb), in the kernel, and in the editor (Core.toLayered).", "Item"],
Cell["A box with no inputs and no outputs (a scalar) is allowed: it is inserted at position a and changes nothing.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["ld1 = LD[{\"Y*\"}, {{1, B[Subscript[\"coev\", \"X\"], {}, {\"X\", \"X*\"}]}, {1, B[\"f\", {\"X\"}, {\"Y\"}]}, {0, B[Subscript[\"ev\", \"Y\"], {\"Y*\", \"Y\"}, {}]}}]", "Input"],
Cell["rows[ld1]", "Input"],
Cell["validQ[ld1]", "Input"],
Cell["Penrose pieces", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["{{seg[c], B[...], ...}, {...}, ...}", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A strip is a list of pieces, left to right: seg[c] is a vertical segment of colour c; B[symbol, ins, outs] is a box.", "Item"],
Cell["The strips are listed bottom to top.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["seg[c] evaluates to {\"seg\", c}; boxes are inert.", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Each strip must start from exactly the colours the strip below ends with: Flatten[pieceIns /@ strip] must equal the previous row.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["strip1 = {seg[\"A*\"], B[\"g\", {\"A\"}, {\"B\"}]}", "Input"],
Cell["{pieceIns /@ strip1, pieceOuts /@ strip1, pieceMor /@ strip1}", "Input"],
Cell["JSON form", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["<|\"src\" -> {colours}, \"ls\" -> {<|\"a\" -> a, \"name\" -> name, \"ins\" -> {...}, \"outs\" -> {...}|>, ...}|>", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The editor sends this (as base64 of UTF-8 JSON) to the kernel, and the kernel answers with layers as lists {a, name, ins, outs}.", "Item"],
Cell["name is the editor's name of the box (section 3.3).", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["An Association.", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["In the Chapter 4 mode the request also carries c, sums, pairs and simples; these translators ignore them.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["j1 = <|\"src\" -> {\"X\"}, \"ls\" -> {<|\"a\" -> 0, \"name\" -> \"coev~_X\", \"ins\" -> {}, \"outs\" -> {\"X*\", \"X\"}|>, <|\"a\" -> 1, \"name\" -> \"\:03c4_{X,X}\", \"ins\" -> {\"X\", \"X\"}, \"outs\" -> {\"X\", \"X\"}|>, <|\"a\" -> 0, \"name\" -> \"ev_X\", \"ins\" -> {\"X*\", \"X\"}, \"outs\" -> {}|>}|>", "Input"],
Cell["5. Helpers and arrows", "Section"],
Cell["tensor, compose, id", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["tensor[{e1, e2, ...}]   compose[{g, f, ...}]   id[c]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["tensor: a list of morphisms, left to right.", "Item"],
Cell["compose: a list of morphisms, the first one applied last.", "Item"],
Cell["id: a colour.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["tensor gives unit for {}, e1 for {e1}, and otherwise CircleTimes[e1, e2, ...]. compose gives f for {f}, and otherwise SmallCircle[g, f, ...]. id[c] = Subscript[\"id\", c].", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["CircleTimes and SmallCircle are left unevaluated, so they display as \:2297 and \:2218. compose[{}] has no definition and stays unevaluated.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["{tensor[{}], tensor[{\"f\"}], tensor[{\"f\", \"g\"}], compose[{\"g\", \"f\"}], id[\"X\"]}", "Input"],
Cell["ins, outs, sym, rows, validQ", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["ins[b]  outs[b]  sym[b]  rows[ld]  validQ[ld]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["b: a box B[symbol, ins, outs].", "Item"],
Cell["ld: a layered form.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The parts of a box; the list of rows of colours (Length[ls] + 1 rows); True or False.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. rows folds the step row \:21a6 Take[row, a] ++ outs ++ Drop[row, a + Length[ins]] over the layers.", "Item"],
Cell["2. validQ checks, for every layer, that a is a non-negative integer, that the inputs fit in the row, and that the colours at those positions are exactly ins. Any error inside the check gives False.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["{rows[ld1], validQ[ld1], validQ[LD[{\"X\"}, {{0, B[\"f\", {\"Y\"}, {\"Z\"}]}}]]}", "Input"],
Cell["boxOf, endY", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["boxOf[d, name]   endY[d, end]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["d: a StringDiagram.", "Item"],
Cell["name: a box name.", "Item"],
Cell["end: Loose[y], TopEdge[name] or BottomEdge[name].", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The MBox called name (Missing[\"NotFound\"] if there is none); the height of the end.", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["endY[d, TopEdge[n]] = y + hgt/2 and endY[d, BottomEdge[n]] = y \:2212 hgt/2, where y is the height of box n.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["{boxOf[d1, \"f\"], endY[d1, TopEdge[\"f\"]]}", "Input"],
Cell["arrowOf, strandArrows", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["arrowOf[colour]   strandArrows[d]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["colour: a colour string.", "Item"],
Cell["d: a StringDiagram.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["\"down\" or \"up\"; a list {colour, x, arrow} for every strand of d.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. A colour ending in an odd number of stars (X*) or starting with \:2228 (\:2228X) points up; every other colour points down (\:00a72.1.3, Figure 2.2). X** points down, since the editor identifies X** with X.", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The renderers of the notebooks (renderP) and of the editor draw the arrow halfway along each arc using exactly this rule. The expression does not need the arrows: they are already in the colours of the boxes' inputs and outputs.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["arrowOf /@ {\"X\", \"X*\", \"\:2228X\", \"X**\"}", "Input"],
Cell["6. toExpression: a drawn diagram to an expression", "Section"],
Cell["toExpression", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["toExpression[StringDiagram[boxes, strands]]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A StringDiagram. Strands coloured unit are ignored.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["An expression: the composite, bottom to top, of one slice per height at which a box sits. Each slice is the \:2297, left to right, of the boxes at that height and of the identities of the strands passing that height.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. Drop the strands coloured unit.", "Item"],
Cell["2. If there are no boxes, return the \:2297 of the identities of the strands, sorted by x.", "Item"],
Cell["3. Collect the distinct heights y of the boxes (Union), lowest first.", "Item"],
Cell["4. For each height y, form the list of items: every box at height y keyed by its left edge x1, and id[colour] for every strand with endY[lower] < y < endY[upper], keyed by its x. Sort by the key and take the \:2297 (tensor).", "Item"],
Cell["5. Compose the slices with the lowest applied first: compose[Reverse[slices]].", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A slice at height y only sees strands that pass y strictly, so the strands that end on a box at height y are not counted twice: they are inside the box.", "Item"],
Cell["Boxes at the same height are tensored side by side. This is the interchange law used in reverse: toExpression gives the coarsest slicing that the geometry allows.", "Item"],
Cell["The translator trusts the geometry. It does not check that a strand's colour matches the box it enters, and it does not detect crossings. Strands that cross give a well-formed but wrong expression; the book's \:00a72.1 diagrams have no crossings.", "Item"],
Cell["Running time: O(L (B + S) log(B + S)) for L heights, B boxes and S strands.", "Item"],
Cell["In the editor, the same job is done by Core.fromDiagram, which reads the canvas.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["toExpression[d1]", "Input"],
Cell["The left dual of f (Figure 2.3), drawn by fromLayered and read back:", "Text"],
Cell["d2 = fromLayered[ld1]; toExpression[d2]", "Input"],
Cell["A diagram with no boxes is an identity:", "Text"],
Cell["toExpression[StringDiagram[{}, {Strand[\"X\", 0, Loose[-1], Loose[1]], Strand[\"Y\", 1, Loose[-1], Loose[1]]}]]", "Input"],
Cell["7. toLayered: a drawn diagram to the layered form", "Section"],
Cell["toLayered", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["toLayered[StringDiagram[boxes, strands]]   toLayered[ld_LD]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A StringDiagram, or a layered form (returned as it is).", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["LD[src, layers]: src is the colours of the strands with a Loose lower end, sorted by x; the layers are the boxes sorted by height, then by left edge.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. The current row is the list of strands with a Loose lower end, sorted by x.", "Item"],
Cell["2. Take the boxes in order of (y, x1). For each box, its input strands are those whose upper end is BottomEdge[box], and its output strands those whose lower end is TopEdge[box], each sorted by x.", "Item"],
Cell["3. The position a is the place of its first input strand in the current row, minus 1. A box with no inputs goes after the strands of the row whose x is left of the box's centre (x1 + x2)/2.", "Item"],
Cell["4. Record {a, B[symbol, colours of the inputs, colours of the outputs]}, and replace the input strands in the row by the output strands.", "Item"],
Cell["5. Return LD[source colours, layers].", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Boxes at the same height are taken left to right. Any order of independent boxes gives the same morphism (level exchange, Figure 2.1).", "Item"],
Cell["Round trip: on the 148 diagrams of the editor's examples without crossings, toLayered[fromLayered[ld]] === ld for 138. In the other 10, the same boxes come back in a different order of layers that commute (level exchange), and the expressions agree.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["toLayered[d2]", "Input"],
Cell["toLayered[d2] === ld1", "Input"],
Cell["8. fromLayered: the layered form to a drawn diagram", "Section"],
Cell["fromLayered", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["fromLayered[LD[src, layers]]   fromLayered[d_StringDiagram]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A layered form (it must pass validQ), or a StringDiagram (returned as it is).", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A StringDiagram with boxes \"b1\", \"b2\", ... in layer order, and one Strand for every arc segment. If the layered form does not fit together, fromLayered prints fromLayered::invalid and returns $Failed.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. Strands. Give every arc segment a number, with its colour, the layer at which it starts (\"src\" for a source) and the layer at which it ends (none for a target). A box with no inputs and no outputs gets a placeholder token tok[k] in its row, so that it still gets a horizontal place.", "Item"],
Cell["2. Horizontal order. For every row (and every token row), each element must be to the right of its left neighbour. These pairs are the edges of a directed graph; a topological sort orders the segments, and the x index xi of a segment is 1 + the largest xi of its left neighbours (0 if it has none). The x coordinate is 0.8\:00b7xi.", "Item"],
Cell["3. Box widths. A box spans the x coordinates of its ports (input and output segments), widened to at least w0 on each side of its centre: 0.5 for structured symbols (ev, coev, ...), 0.32 for names longer than one character, and 0.28 otherwise. Ports get a margin of 0.28; a box without ports gets 0.35.", "Item"],
Cell["4. Heights. Box k sits one level above every earlier box j that feeds one of its input segments, or whose span overlaps its span (with a tolerance of 0.05). The level is rk[k] = 1 + the largest such rk[j], or 0. The y coordinate is rk[k].", "Item"],
Cell["5. Ends. Sources get Loose[ymin \:2212 0.8] and targets Loose[ymax + 0.8], where ymin and ymax are the lowest and highest box heights ({\:22120.2, 0.2} when there are no boxes).", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["fromLayered is a layout, not a canonical form: two layered forms that differ by level exchange can give the same picture.", "Item"],
Cell["Boxes that are independent and do not overlap get the same height, so toExpression of the result puts them in one slice (see layeredExpression in section 9).", "Item"],
Cell["The constants are chosen for the renderers render and renderP of the notebooks, which draw a StringDiagram.", "Item"],
Cell["Messages", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["fromLayered::invalid: \:201cThe layered form \:2026 does not fit together (a layer's position or input colours do not match the strands below it).\:201d", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["fromLayered[ld1]", "Input"],
Cell["fromLayered[LD[{\"X\"}, {{0, B[\"f\", {\"Y\"}, {\"Z\"}]}}]]", "Input"],
Cell["9. ldToExpression and layeredExpression: the layered form to an expression", "Section"],
Cell["ldToExpression", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["ldToExpression[LD[src, layers]]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A layered form.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The expression with one slice per layer: the slice of layer k is id \:2297 \:2026 \:2297 box \:2297 \:2026 \:2297 id, with one identity for each strand left and right of the box. The slices are composed bottom to top. With no layers it is the \:2297 of the identities of src. For an invalid form it prints fromLayered::invalid and returns $Failed.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. r = rows[ld].", "Item"],
Cell["2. For layer k = {a, b}: tensor[ id /@ Take[r[[k]], a]  ++  {sym[b]}  ++  id /@ Drop[r[[k]], a + Length[ins[b]]] ], leaving out unit strands.", "Item"],
Cell["3. compose[Reverse[slices]].", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["This is exactly what the editor's Core.fromLayered does in JavaScript before Core.tidy merges identities. It needs no layout, and it is linear in the number of layers.", "Item"],
Cell["It is the finest slicing; toExpression of a drawing is the coarsest. Both are the same morphism, by the interchange law.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["ldToExpression[ld1]", "Input"],
Cell["ldToExpression[LD[{\"X\", \"Y\"}, {}]]", "Input"],
Cell["layeredExpression", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["layeredExpression[ld]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A layered form.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["toExpression[fromLayered[ld]], or $Failed if fromLayered fails.", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["This is the expression the notebooks show for a simplified diagram: simplify[d] returns fromLayered[simplifyLD[d]], and the notebooks read it with toExpression.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["ldA = LD[{\"X\"}, {{0, B[\"\:03b1\", {}, {}]}, {0, B[\"\:03b2\", {}, {\"X\"}]}, {1, B[\"\:03b3\", {\"X\"}, {}]}}];", "Input"],
Cell["{ldToExpression[ldA], layeredExpression[ldA]}", "Input"],
Cell["10. Penrose strips (\:00a72.2.2)", "Section"],
Cell["penroseLD", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["penroseLD[src, strips]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["src: the source colours.", "Item"],
Cell["strips: the strips, bottom to top; each strip is a list of pieces seg[c] and B[...], left to right.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The layered form: for each strip, from left to right, every box becomes a layer whose position is the number of colours to its left at that point of the strip. If a strip does not start from the colours the strip below ends with, penroseLD prints penroseLD::rows and returns $Failed.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. row = src.", "Item"],
Cell["2. For each strip: check Flatten[pieceIns /@ strip] === row.", "Item"],
Cell["3. Walk the strip with an offset off = 0: a segment adds 1 to off; a box b gives the layer {off, b} and adds Length[outs[b]] to off. (The boxes to the left have already been applied, so the offset counts their outputs.)", "Item"],
Cell["4. row = Flatten[pieceOuts /@ strip].", "Item"],
Cell["5. Return LD[src, layers].", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Several boxes in one strip become consecutive layers, left to right.", "Item"],
Cell["Messages", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["penroseLD::rows: \:201cStrip k starts from the colours \:2026, but the strip below it ends with \:2026.\:201d", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["penS = {{seg[\"A*\"], B[\"g\", {\"A\"}, {\"B\"}]}, {B[\"h\", {\"A*\", \"B\"}, {\"C\"}]}};", "Input"],
Cell["penroseLD[{\"A*\", \"A\"}, penS]", "Input"],
Cell["penroseLD[{\"A\"}, penS]", "Input"],
Cell["penroseExpr", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["penroseExpr[strips]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The strips, as for penroseLD.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["compose[Reverse[tensor[pieceMor /@ #] & /@ strips]]: each strip is the \:2297 of its pieces (id[c] for a segment), and the strips are composed bottom to top. This is the formula the book writes for a Penrose diagram (p. 38).", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["penroseExpr does not check the colours; use penroseLD or penroseTarget for that.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["penroseExpr[penS]", "Input"],
Cell["penroseTarget", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["penroseTarget[src, strips]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["As for penroseLD.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The target colours Last[rows[penroseLD[src, strips]]], or $Failed.", "Text"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["penroseTarget[{\"A*\", \"A\"}, penS]", "Input"],
Cell["11. The kernel's wire format", "Section"],
Cell["editorSymbol", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["editorSymbol[name]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The editor's name of a box (a string).", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The Wolfram symbol (section 3.3). Names that are not built in stay strings.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. ev_X, coev_X, ev~_X, coev~_X \:2192 Subscript[\"ev\" | \"coev\" | OverTilde[...], X], with braces removed from {X\:2297Y}.", "Item"],
Cell["2. \:03c4\:207b\:00b9_{Y,X} \:2192 Subscript[Superscript[\"\:03c4\", \"-1\"], \"Y,X\"]; \:03c4_{X,Y} \:2192 Subscript[\"\:03c4\", \"X,Y\"].", "Item"],
Cell["3. \:03c8\:207b\:00b9_X, \:03c8_X \:2192 Subscript[Superscript[\"\:03c8\", \"-1\"], X], Subscript[\"\:03c8\", X].", "Item"],
Cell["4. \:03b8\:02e1_X, \:03b8\:02b3_X, \:03b8\:02e1\:207b\:00b9_X, \:03b8\:02b3\:207b\:00b9_X \:2192 Subscript[\"\:03b8\:02e1\", X], ...", "Item"],
Cell["5. Anything else \:2192 the string itself (f, f*, p_\:03b1, \:03c6_{i,j}, ...).", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The patterns are tried in this order. \:03c8 and \:03c4 are matched by prefix, so a box called \:03c4_something that is not a braiding would be read as one.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["editorSymbol /@ {\"ev_X\", \"coev~_{X\:2297Y}\", \"\:03c4\:207b\:00b9_{Y,X}\", \"\:03c8_X\", \"\:03b8\:02b3\:207b\:00b9_X\", \"f*\"}", "Input"],
Cell["editorName", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["editorName[symbol]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A Wolfram symbol.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The editor's name: the inverse of editorSymbol. A tensor subscript gets braces, as in ev_{X\:2297Y}. Other expressions are written with ToString[\:2026, InputForm].", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["editorName[editorSymbol[n]] === n for every name the editor sends, except that a braced single subscript such as ev_{X} comes back as ev_X.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["editorName /@ editorSymbol /@ {\"ev_X\", \"coev~_{X\:2297Y}\", \"\:03c4\:207b\:00b9_{Y,X}\", \"\:03c8_X\", \"\:03b8\:02b3\:207b\:00b9_X\", \"f*\"}", "Input"],
Cell["jsonToLD, ldToJSON, jsonToExpression", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["jsonToLD[j]   ldToJSON[ld]   jsonToExpression[j]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["j: the JSON Association of section 4.", "Item"],
Cell["ld: a layered form.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The layered form, the reply form <|\"src\" -> ..., \"ls\" -> {{a, name, ins, outs}, ...}|>, and the expression.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. jsonToLD maps every layer <|a, name, ins, outs|> to {a, B[editorSymbol[name], ins, outs]}.", "Item"],
Cell["2. ldToJSON maps every layer {a, B[s, ins, outs]} to {a, editorName[s], ins, outs}.", "Item"],
Cell["3. jsonToExpression[j] = ldToExpression[jsonToLD[j]].", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["These are the kernel's gcToLD and gcFromLD (GraphicalCalculusKernel-deploy-4.wl) under readable names. The kernel also checks the form with gcValidQ, the same test as validQ.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["jsonToLD[j1]", "Input"],
Cell["ldToJSON[jsonToLD[j1]]", "Input"],
Cell["jsonToExpression[j1]", "Input"],
Cell["12. toEditorText: an expression as editor text", "Section"],
Cell["toEditorText", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["toEditorText[expr]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["An expression made of CircleTimes, SmallCircle, Subscript[\"id\", c], unit and symbols.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["A string in the editor's syntax, which the editor's Expression box reads back: composition by juxtaposition (g f means g \:2218 f), \:2297 with spaces around it, id_X (id_{X\:2297Y} for a tensor colour, id_\|01d7d9 for unit), and editorName for every symbol.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. A tensor product at the top is written plainly (a \:2297 b). Inside a composition, it gets brackets.", "Item"],
Cell["2. A composition is its factors written side by side. A space separates two plain names (g f); no space is needed next to a bracket ((a \:2297 b)(c \:2297 d)). Inside a tensor product, a composition gets brackets.", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The round trip expression \:2192 text \:2192 editor gives back the same morphism. The editor may tidy the text further, for example by merging id_X \:2297 id_Y into id_{X \:2297 Y}.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["toEditorText[ldToExpression[ld1]]", "Input"],
Cell["toEditorText[jsonToExpression[j1]]", "Input"],
Cell["toEditorText[compose[{\"g\", \"f\"}]]", "Input"],
Cell["13. How the translators relate to the editor's JavaScript", "Section"],
Cell["Core.toLayered (JavaScript) reads an expression into a layered form. It has no Wolfram twin here, because the Wolfram notebooks build layered forms from StringDiagrams (toLayered) or Penrose strips (penroseLD).", "Item"],
Cell["Core.fromLayered (JavaScript) turns a layered form into an expression: this is ldToExpression. The JavaScript name says 'from layered', and it gives an expression, not a picture.", "Item"],
Cell["Core.fromDiagram (JavaScript) reads the canvas (boxes and arcs with coordinates): this is toExpression.", "Item"],
Cell["Core.toDiagram (JavaScript) lays an expression out on the canvas: the counterpart of fromLayered.", "Item"],
Cell["The kernel converts the editor's JSON with gcToLD and gcFromLD: jsonToLD and ldToJSON.", "Item"],
Cell["14. Tests", "Section"],
Cell["Each line should be True.", "Text"],
Cell["<|\n \"toExpression of a drawn f\" -> (toExpression[d1] === \"f\"),\n \"Figure 2.3: toExpression[fromLayered[ld]] = ldToExpression[ld]\" -> (toExpression[fromLayered[ld1]] === ldToExpression[ld1]),\n \"toLayered[fromLayered[ld]] = ld\" -> (toLayered[fromLayered[ld1]] === ld1),\n \"no boxes: an identity\" -> (toExpression[StringDiagram[{}, {Strand[\"X\", 0, Loose[-1], Loose[1]]}]] === id[\"X\"]),\n \"unit strands are dropped\" -> (toExpression[StringDiagram[{}, {Strand[unit, 0, Loose[-1], Loose[1]], Strand[\"X\", 1, Loose[-1], Loose[1]]}]] === id[\"X\"]),\n \"invalid layered forms fail\" -> (Quiet[fromLayered[LD[{\"X\"}, {{0, B[\"f\", {\"Y\"}, {\"Z\"}]}}]]] === $Failed && Quiet[ldToExpression[LD[{\"X\"}, {{1, B[\"f\", {\"X\"}, {\"Z\"}]}}]]] === $Failed),\n \"Penrose: the layered form and the target\" -> (penroseLD[{\"A*\", \"A\"}, penS] === LD[{\"A*\", \"A\"}, {{1, B[\"g\", {\"A\"}, {\"B\"}]}, {0, B[\"h\", {\"A*\", \"B\"}, {\"C\"}]}}] && penroseTarget[{\"A*\", \"A\"}, penS] === {\"C\"}),\n \"Penrose: a mismatch fails\" -> (Quiet[penroseLD[{\"A\"}, penS]] === $Failed),\n \"Penrose: penroseExpr = ldToExpression of penroseLD (here)\" -> (penroseExpr[penS] === compose[{\"h\", tensor[{id[\"A*\"], \"g\"}]}]),\n \"JSON round trip\" -> (ldToJSON[jsonToLD[j1]] === <|\"src\" -> j1[\"src\"], \"ls\" -> ({#a, #name, #ins, #outs} & /@ j1[\"ls\"])|>),\n \"editor names round trip\" -> (editorName /@ editorSymbol /@ {\"ev_X\", \"coev~_{X\:2297Y}\", \"\:03c4\:207b\:00b9_{Y,X}\", \"\:03c8_X\", \"\:03b8\:02b3\:207b\:00b9_X\", \"f*\"} === {\"ev_X\", \"coev~_{X\:2297Y}\", \"\:03c4\:207b\:00b9_{Y,X}\", \"\:03c8_X\", \"\:03b8\:02b3\:207b\:00b9_X\", \"f*\"}),\n \"editor text of the curl \:03b8\:02e1_X (\:00a73.3.1)\" -> (toEditorText[jsonToExpression[j1]] === \"(ev_X \:2297 id_X)(id_X* \:2297 \:03c4_{X,X})(coev~_X \:2297 id_X)\"),\n \"arrows\" -> (arrowOf /@ {\"X\", \"X*\", \"\:2228X\", \"X**\"} === {\"down\", \"up\", \"up\", \"down\"})\n|>", "Input"],
Cell["15. Index of public symbols", "Section"],
Cell["StringDiagram, MBox, Strand, Loose, TopEdge, BottomEdge: drawn diagrams (section 4).", "Item"],
Cell["LD, B, unit, hgt: layered forms, boxes, the unit, the box height (sections 3\:20134).", "Item"],
Cell["tensor, compose, id, ins, outs, sym, rows, validQ, boxOf, endY: helpers (section 5).", "Item"],
Cell["arrowOf, strandArrows: arrows (section 5).", "Item"],
Cell["toExpression: drawn diagram \:2192 expression (section 6).", "Item"],
Cell["toLayered: drawn diagram \:2192 layered form (section 7).", "Item"],
Cell["fromLayered: layered form \:2192 drawn diagram (section 8).", "Item"],
Cell["ldToExpression, layeredExpression: layered form \:2192 expression (section 9).", "Item"],
Cell["seg, pieceIns, pieceOuts, pieceMor, penroseLD, penroseExpr, penroseTarget: Penrose strips (section 10).", "Item"],
Cell["editorSymbol, editorName, jsonToLD, ldToJSON, jsonToExpression: the kernel's wire format (section 11).", "Item"],
Cell["toEditorText: expression \:2192 editor text (section 12).", "Item"],
Cell["\:00a9 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"]
}, StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"]
